    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Land subsidence is the lowering of the ground surface, documented across Iranian aquifers affected by groundwater extraction. Satellite research shows that aquifer compaction can create lasting losses of storage capacity as well as uneven ground movement that threatens infrastructure. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Land subsidence", "Groundwater", "Aquifers", "Satellite monitoring", "Water management"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T12:35:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T12:35:27Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Land Subsidence in Iran: Aquifers and Sinking Ground | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s groundwater-related subsidence, how radar satellites measure it, and why uneven movement and irreversible compaction matter."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "compaction-and-storage",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A water problem with a physical legacy",
        "content": "Land subsidence makes groundwater depletion visible at the surface. In Iran, satellite studies link extensive sinking ground to water extraction and aquifer compaction. The concern extends beyond a lower water table: where sediments compact irreversibly, the aquifer loses some of its capacity to store water. Subsequent rainfall cannot simply restore the original underground structure. [s1][s3]\n\nResearch distinguishes recoverable, elastic movement from lasting, inelastic deformation. Seasonal movement can reflect changes in groundwater conditions, while persistent compaction records a different kind of damage. A countrywide study published in 2025 found widespread irrecoverable deformation and examined how drought affected the balance between recoverable and permanent movement. These processes connect subsidence to drought without making them identical hazards. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "geographic-patterns",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the national maps",
        "content": "A 2024 Science Advances study used Sentinel-1 satellite observations from 2014–2020 to map subsidence across Iran. It associated much of the affected land with irrigation and identified the central plateau as an important concentration of groundwater depletion. The findings describe spatial patterns over an observation interval, rather than a single year’s newly affected land. [s2]\n\nA later study analyzed observations from 2014–2022, separating vertical and east–west surface motion. Its mapped subsidence regions were defined using an explicit rate threshold. The studies’ differing methods, periods, and inclusion criteria mean their mapped areas cannot be placed side by side as evidence that the national hazard expanded or contracted between publication dates. A valid trend comparison would require consistent definitions and repeated measurements. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "infrastructure-and-local-variation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why uneven movement matters",
        "content": "Earlier work around Tehran demonstrated that the problem had developed over a much longer period. Researchers at the German Research Centre for Geosciences described persistent deformation, ground fissures, and cracks in buildings, alongside irreversible damage to parts of the aquifer system. Their explanation connected groundwater demand with extraction and declining water levels. [s3]\n\nDamage risk depends partly on differences in movement between neighboring locations. The 2025 countrywide research evaluated differential subsidence gradients when assessing exposure. A regional average can therefore conceal important local conditions: two places sinking at similar average rates need not have the same pattern of distortion. Such maps support regional assessment, but they do not certify the structural condition of an individual building. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "measurement-and-response",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What satellites can establish",
        "content": "Interferometric Synthetic Aperture Radar, usually shortened to InSAR, compares radar observations acquired on different dates to detect surface displacement. The COMET-LiCS Land Subsidence Portal explains how repeated measurements reveal spatial patterns and changes over time. It complements ground-based techniques, which provide precise measurements at fewer locations. [s4]\n\nMonitoring establishes where deformation occurs and how it develops; addressing its groundwater drivers requires water-management decisions. The scientific value of the maps lies in connecting measured surface movement with aquifer conditions and exposed settlements. Their central warning is that a water deficit can leave a lasting geological legacy, even when a later season brings better rainfall. [s1][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
